From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Your Name <you@example.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix loss of DWT cycles in the s32k148evb system timer

updateTicks() converted the elapsed DWT cycles into ticks and then set
lastDwt to the current DWT value. The cycles that did not add up to a
whole tick were dropped, so every call lost up to dwtTicksRatio - 1
cycles (4 of 5 at 80 MHz, 2 of 3 at 48 MHz).

The loss is not a fixed offset: it grows with the number of calls.
updateTicks() runs on every getSystemTicks/getSystemTimeUs/... call, so
the system time runs slow at a rate that depends on how often the
application reads it, and a caller polling faster than one tick can
even see the time stand still.

lastDwt now advances only by the cycles that became whole ticks, so the
remainder is counted by the next call. This is what the stm32 platform
already does in platforms/stm32/bsp/bspTimer/src/bsp/SystemTimer/
SystemTimer.cpp.

Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>
---
 .../src/bsp/SystemTimer/SystemTimer.cpp                | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/bspConfiguration/src/bsp/SystemTimer/SystemTimer.cpp b/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/bspConfiguration/src/bsp/SystemTimer/SystemTimer.cpp
index 4eba0fec..c7a60b5e 100644
--- a/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/bspConfiguration/src/bsp/SystemTimer/SystemTimer.cpp
+++ b/executables/referenceApp/platforms/s32k148evb/bspConfiguration/src/bsp/SystemTimer/SystemTimer.cpp
@@ -44,9 +44,13 @@ uint32_t volatile& DEMCR      = *reinterpret_cast<uint32_t volatile*>(0xE000EDFC
 uint64_t updateTicks()
 {
     ETL_MAYBE_UNUSED const interrupts::SuspendResumeAllInterruptsScopedLock lock;
-    uint32_t const curDwt = DWT_CYCCNT;
-    state.ticks += static_cast<uint32_t>((curDwt - state.lastDwt) / state.dwtTicksRatio);
-    state.lastDwt = curDwt;
+    uint32_t const curDwt   = DWT_CYCCNT;
+    uint32_t const newTicks = (curDwt - state.lastDwt) / state.dwtTicksRatio;
+    state.ticks += newTicks;
+    // Consume only the cycles that became whole ticks so the remainder carries over to the next
+    // call. Setting lastDwt to curDwt would discard up to dwtTicksRatio - 1 cycles per call, which
+    // makes the clock run slow at a rate that depends on how often it is read.
+    state.lastDwt += newTicks * state.dwtTicksRatio;
     return state.ticks;
 }
 
